---
import ToolLayout from '../../layouts/ToolLayout.astro';
import TextStats from '../../components/TextStats.jsx';
---
<ToolLayout title="Text Statistics Analyzer" description="Get word count, sentence count, average word/sentence length, and estimated reading time for any text.">
  <TextStats client:load slot="tool" />

  <h2>Why average sentence length is a useful readability signal</h2>
  <p>Very long average sentence length often correlates with harder-to-read text, even before considering vocabulary — it's a quick, objective proxy for readability that's worth checking alongside more subjective editorial judgment.</p>

  <h2>How to use this analyzer</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Paste your text.</li>
    <li>Review word count, sentence count, and average length statistics.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Common Text Stats Mistakes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Treating reading time estimates as precise</strong> — the 200 words/minute baseline is a rough average; actual reading speed varies significantly by reader and content complexity.</li>
    <li><strong>Optimizing purely for shorter sentences</strong> without considering whether the resulting choppy style actually reads better for the specific content.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>When to Use This Text Stats Analyzer</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Checking estimated reading time before publishing a blog post or article</li>
    <li>Getting a quick readability signal via average sentence length</li>
    <li>Comparing statistics across drafts to see if edits improved conciseness</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Text Statistics FAQ</h2>
  <p><strong>Is 200 words/minute accurate for everyone?</strong> It's a commonly used average — actual reading speed varies by individual and by how technical or dense the content is.</p>
</ToolLayout>
